A script defines `pub fn run() -> String`. What it returns is reported back. A function is `fn name(a: i64, b: f64) -> i64 = { ... }`, with an `=` before the body.

```frust
fn twice(a: i64) -> i64 = {
    a * 2
}

pub fn run() -> String = {
    station_log_i64("twice 4", twice(4));
    "done"
}
```

Blocks and semicolons. Inside `{ ... }`, statements are separated by `;`. The last expression, with no `;`, is the value of the block. This includes `if`, `else`, `while`, `for` and `match` blocks: when something follows them, the closing brace needs a `;`.

```frust
pub fn run() -> String = {
    let track = station_track_add("Bass");
    if (track == 0) {
        return "could not add the track";
    };
    "Added Bass"
}
```

Without the `};` after the `if`, the compiler reports "unexpected ... expecting }" on the NEXT line, so the real mistake is on the line before the one reported.

An `if`/`else` as the last thing in a function is its value, so it has no `;` and each branch gives a value of the same kind.

```frust
pub fn run() -> String = {
    let ok = station_track_set_volume_db(1, -6.0);
    if (ok == 0) {
        station_last_error()
    } else {
        "Set the volume"
    }
}
```

Variables. `let` makes a variable; only one made with `let mut` can be assigned again with `=`. Convert a whole number to a decimal with `as`: `n as f64`.

```frust
pub fn run() -> String = {
    let count = station_track_count();
    let mut total = 0;
    total = total + count;
    station_log_i64("total", total);
    "done"
}
```

```frust
pub fn run() -> String = {
    let a = station_track_count();
    if ((a > 0) & (a < 5)) {
        "between one and four tracks"
    } else {
        "some other number"
    }
}
```

Chained conditions and loops. A loop is followed by `;` when more code comes after it.

```frust
pub fn run() -> String = {
    let count = station_track_count();
    if (count == 0) {
        "no tracks"
    } else if (count == 1) {
        "one track"
    } else {
        "several tracks"
    }
}
```

```frust
pub fn run() -> String = {
    let mut track = 1;
    while (track <= station_track_count()) {
        station_track_set_muted(track, 1);
        track = track + 1;
    };
    for number in 1..3 {
        station_log_i64("number", number);
    };
    "muted every track"
}
```

`for name in a..b { ... }` counts from a up to but not including b. `loop { ... }` repeats until `break`; `continue` skips to the next round; `return value;` leaves the function early.

Structs, enums and match.

```frust
struct Point { x: f64, y: f64 }

pub fn run() -> String = {
    let p = Point { x: 1.0, y: 2.0 };
    let total = p.x + p.y;
    "ok"
}
```

```frust
enum Shape { Circle(f64), Empty }

fn area(s: Shape) -> f64 = {
    match (s) {
        Shape::Circle(r) => r * r * 3.0;
        Shape::Empty => 0.0
    }
}

pub fn run() -> String = {
    let a = area(Shape::Circle(2.0));
    "ok"
}
```

```frust
pub fn run() -> String = {
    let n = 2;
    match (n) {
        1 => "one";
        2 => "two";
        _ => "many"
    }
}
```

Methods: `impl Name { fn method(self) -> i64 = { self.field + 1 } }` adds methods to a struct, called as `value.method()`.

```frust
struct Counter { n: i64 }

impl Counter {
    fn bump(self) -> i64 = { self.n + 1 }
}

pub fn run() -> String = {
    let c = Counter { n: 1 };
    let k = c.bump();
    "ok"
}
```
